CORE
main.cpp
--cpp98
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ derived and base pointers: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ with qualification: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ first base: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [expr.eq]/3, [expr.type]/4: in `pr == &m' (R * and M *, M derived
from R) both operands are converted to the composite pointer type R *, so
&m is adjusted to its R subobject and the comparison is true.  The C rule
used for the comparison cast the M * to R * without adjustment, so it was
false for a base at a non-zero offset (bin53).  typecheck_expr_rel now
applies the derived-to-base conversion first.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18
accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
